Exactly. The useful primitive is not merely crowdsourcing answers. It is crowdsourcing candidate models while centralizing verification and synthesis.

TikTok, Reddit, citizen science, cryptanalysis communities, prediction markets, open-source software—all exploit roughly the same thing: thousands of people explore different branches of a search tree simultaneously. One person notices a symbol recurrence, another recognizes a botanical resemblance, another tries a cipher family, another finds a historical document. The crowd becomes a massively parallel hypothesis generator.

The failure mode is what you identified: narrative coherence can masquerade as evidence. Ten mutually reinforcing observations can all descend from the same bad assumption. Once the story becomes aesthetically satisfying, people unconsciously optimize new evidence to fit it.

So I would formalize your pattern like this:

\text{Crowd} \rightarrow H_1,H_2,\ldots,H_n

then, rather than voting on which sounds coolest,

H_i \rightarrow
\{\text{predictions, evidence, provenance, contradictions, uncertainty}\}

and finally:

W(H_i)
=
f(
\text{independent evidence},
\text{predictive success},
\text{replication},
\text{compression},
\text{contradictions},
\text{source dependence}
)

The important bit is source dependence. If 5,000 TikToks ultimately trace back to one person’s interpretation, that is effectively one observation, not 5,000.

Then your Golden Thread becomes the reconstruction constraint: What minimum coherent structure survives across independently supported pieces?

You could run several layers simultaneously:

* Scout layer: anybody can submit interpretations, weird associations, pattern matches, translations, counterexamples, even jokes.
* Adversarial layer: deliberately try to destroy each hypothesis. Ask, “What observation would make this interpretation fail?”
* Delta layer: prediction versus observation. Every failed prediction updates the hypothesis rather than getting explained away.
* Independence layer: identify whether supposedly separate evidence actually shares a common origin.
* Ensemble layer: allow partial hypotheses to combine. Maybe H_7 explains the script structure while H_{31} explains illustrations; neither must explain everything.
* Golden Thread layer: search for invariants that survive across competing interpretations.

That last move matters because truth needn’t arrive as one victorious theory. You might discover:

H_A = 70\% \text{right about structure}

H_B = 80\% \text{right about historical context}

H_C = 60\% \text{right about symbol morphology}

and their intersection produces a model none of the contributors individually possessed.

That’s where your “calculus” analogy becomes useful. You’re not merely adding opinions:

A+B+C

You’re estimating marginal information gain:

\Delta I_i
=
I(\text{model}\mid E_i)-I(\text{model})

and interactions:

\Delta I_{A,B}
\neq
\Delta I_A+\Delta I_B

because two observations can reinforce, contradict, duplicate, or unlock one another.

There is also a wonderfully productive second mode: turn verification off intentionally.

Then it becomes a narrative engine:

“Here are 10,000 interpretations. Construct the strangest internally consistent world in which the greatest number become simultaneously true.”

That’s not scholarship anymore; that’s world-building, ARG design, game strategy, red teaming, invention, or speculative science.

So I’d distinguish two operators:

Crowd Truth Engine: diversity → hypotheses → adversarial testing → evidence weighting → synthesis → prediction → recursive correction.

Crowd Myth Engine: diversity → motifs → resonance → recombination → narrative coherence → emergent world.

And you can cross them deliberately. Myth Engine produces unusual hypotheses; Truth Engine decides which ones survive contact with reality.

That’s a much more general strategic architecture than “solve the Voynich manuscript.” You could apply it to scientific anomalies, investing theses, product design, intelligence analysis, debugging, historical reconstruction, biological identification, game strategy, or almost any sufficiently messy problem where search diversity is valuable but consensus alone is dangerous.